// Phorma Agent Studio - telemetry admin dashboard.
//
// A STATIC ASSET Worker. It has no `main`, no bindings and no secrets: the
// dashboard is a browser app that talks to the authenticated
// `/api/admin/telemetry/*` routes on the `phorma-agent-studio-cloud` Worker with
// a Clerk session token. Nothing privileged lives here.
//
// THE CUSTOM DOMAIN IS NOT OPTIONAL, and this is the one thing to understand
// before changing anything here.
//
// A browser origin that is CROSS-SITE with the Clerk Frontend API
// (`clerk.phorma-labs.com`) can never hold a Clerk session on a PRODUCTION
// instance - the session cookie is first-party to the FAPI's registrable domain.
// This repo already relies on that fact elsewhere: it is the entire reason
// `GET /auth/device` exists on the cloud Worker rather than the studio's webview
// signing in directly (see `device-auth-routes.ts`).
//
// `phorma-admin.phorma-agent-studio.workers.dev` is on `workers.dev`, a different
// registrable domain from `phorma-labs.com`, so it is in exactly that position.
// Measured on 2026-09-09: clerk-js loads there (`/v1/environment` answers 200 for
// any origin, so that endpoint proves nothing), the sign-in form renders with
// production branding, and the sign-in attempt then fails with Clerk's
// `authorization_invalid`. The instance also reports
// `url_based_session_syncing: true` and has NO satellite domain configured.
//
// So the dashboard is served from `telemetry-admin.phorma-labs.com`, a subdomain of
// the Clerk primary domain, where cookies are first-party and no satellite handshake
// is involved. The workers.dev origin stays listed in the cloud Worker's origin
// lists because the worker still answers there, but it cannot sign anyone in.
//
// Adding or changing an origin is FOUR steps, and each has a distinct failure:
//   1. here (a `routes` entry with `custom_domain: true`),
//   2. both origin lists in `cloud/wrangler.jsonc`,
//   3. `wrangler deploy --env hosted` - a `vars` change reaches production only on
//      a deploy. Miss this and every request from the origin is a hard 401.
//   4. the Clerk PRODUCTION instance's allowlist. WHICH list depends on the origin:
//      an unrelated host goes in `allowed_origins`
//      (`PATCH https://api.clerk.com/v1/instance`, which REPLACES the array, so a
//      partial list locks out whatever was left off), while a SUBDOMAIN of the
//      primary domain needs the separate ALLOWED SUBDOMAINS list. Miss this and the
//      page renders but nobody can sign in, with no error in our own logs - the
//      credential-free probe below is how to tell.
//
// Probe an origin without any secret:
//   curl -s -X POST "https://clerk.phorma-labs.com/v1/client/sign_ins?_clerk_js_version=5.0.0" \
//        -H "Origin: <candidate>" -H "Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded" --data "strategy="
// 422 `form_param_nil` = allowed, 403 `subdomain_not_allowed` = a primary-domain
// subdomain that is not allowlisted, `origin_invalid` = an unrelated refused origin.
//
// LOCAL `npm start` CANNOT talk to the deployed worker. `ng serve` uses the
// `development` configuration, which has no `fileReplacements`, so it ships the
// `pk_test_` DEV key - while the deployed worker verifies against the PRODUCTION
// Clerk instance. A dev-minted token fails signature verification there and the
// dashboard renders "Session not accepted". For local work, point
// `environment.ts`'s `apiBaseUrl` at a local `wrangler dev --env hosted` carrying
// the DEV `CLERK_SECRET_KEY` and `CLERK_JWT_KEY` in `cloud/.dev.vars`.
//
// Deploy (independent of the installer, but AFTER the cloud Worker when a release
// adds admin routes, so the API exists before the dashboard calls it):
//   1. npx wrangler login
//   2. npm run build      # writes dist/admin/browser, with the pk_live_ key
//   3. npx wrangler deploy
{
  "name": "phorma-admin",
  "compatibility_date": "2025-05-01",
  // `custom_domain: true` has Cloudflare create and manage the DNS record and the
  // certificate for this hostname, which is what a static-asset worker on its own
  // subdomain wants; a plain route pattern would need a record to attach to. The
  // zone `phorma-labs.com` already lives on this account (it serves
  // `cloud.phorma-labs.com`), so there is no zone setup involved.
  "routes": [
    {
      "pattern": "telemetry-admin.phorma-labs.com",
      "custom_domain": true
    }
  ],
  "assets": {
    // The Angular application builder writes the browser bundle here
    // (`outputPath: dist/admin` plus the builder's own `browser` subfolder).
    "directory": "./dist/admin/browser",
    // Client-side routing: unknown paths serve index.html rather than 404.
    "not_found_handling": "single-page-application"
  },
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  }
}
